// Core FixedVec surface — ALLOCATOR STORY STAGE 1 (heap-free).
//
// `FixedVec<T, const N: u64>` is the fixed-capacity vector: a value type
// whose backing store is an inline fixed array `[T; N]` plus a `u64` length.
// There is NO heap and NO allocator anywhere in this type — capacity is part
// of the type, storage lives wherever the value lives, and running out of
// room is a PROOF failure at compile time, never a runtime reallocation or
// trap (allocator story stage 1: every operation that needs room carries a
// `requires` obligation the caller must discharge). `Arena` service reach
// and the `Arena` boundary (core/arena.omg) are untouched by this
// type; they only enter the story at stage 2 (`Vec<'r, T>` borrowing a
// Arena).
//
// GENERIC INSTANTIATION FRONTIER (re-measured 2026-09-21): the generic `data`
// declaration below compiles structurally (chapter 12 const value
// parameters), and so do the boundary-operator contracts. Attaching a machine
// to generic data is no longer the blocker it was in the 2026-06-12 reading:
// a machine attached to a template with no type parameters of its own now
// lays out through its concrete clones, which the layout builder names for
// exactly this case, and both halves are canaried —
// `tests/omega/pass/generics/runtime_container_setter_matrix_exit`
// (`Cell<T>` with `Cell::touch_count(&self)`) and
// `.../runtime_const_container_methods_exit` (`FixedBuffer<const N: u64>`
// with attached accessors). What is still unproven is this file's own shape:
// a field that binds BOTH a type and a const extent, `[T; N]`, carrying an
// attached machine. The three `[T; N]` canaries
// (`const_data_param`, `runtime_const_data_array_length_exit`,
// `runtime_const_data_multiple_instances_exit`) reach those fields only from
// `Main::main`; none attaches a machine to the container. A generic TEMPLATE
// machine also still has no layout of its own — only its concrete clones do —
// so a declaration-only surface like this one, with no concrete
// `FixedVec<i32, 4>` in the program, still emits nothing. The
// machine-shaped implementation pattern (real bodies, real proofs, real
// runtime) is therefore pinned CONCRETELY in
// tests/omega/pass/collections/runtime_fixed_vec_round_trip_exit (i32, N = 4):
// a hand instantiation of exactly the contracts declared here. When generic
// machine instantiation lands, those machine bodies move into this file
// verbatim (modulo `i32` -> `T`, `4` -> `N`).
//
// Contract vocabulary notes (why the obligations are spelled as they are):
//
//   * `push` requires provable spare room (`length < items.len`, where
//     `items.len` folds to the static `N` at concrete instantiations).
//   * `pop` requires a provably non-empty vector. In the concrete machine
//     pattern it additionally spells the predecessor-index bound
//     (`length - 1 < items.len`): with `length <= N` as a maintained data
//     invariant that bound would be derivable from `length > 0`, but stage 1
//     has no data-invariant vocabulary, so the obligation surfaces at the
//     boundary and is discharged by `push`'s guarantees.
//   * `get` requires the index be within the LIVE length, not merely within
//     capacity.
//   * `as_slice` lends the first `length` elements — the view length is the
//     vector length, not the capacity — so it carries the window obligation
//     `length <= items.len`.

pub data FixedVec<T, const N: u64> {
    items: [T; N];
    length: u64;
}

// Operation contracts, vec.omg-style boundary-operator surface. These carry
// the proof-facing requires/ensures; the machine-shaped implementations are
// blocked on the generic-machine frontier above and live concretely in the
// runtime canary until instantiation lands.

// Length in LIVE elements (`length`, not the static capacity `N`). Cheap
// O(1) measure for `requires`/`ensures`.
boundary operator FixedVec::Length<T, const N: u64>(items: &FixedVec<T, N>) -> u64;

// Append a value. Requires provable spare room — there is no growth path and
// no trap; a call without a discharged capacity fact is a compile error.
boundary operator FixedVec::push<T, const N: u64>(items: &mut FixedVec<T, N>, value: T) -> ()
requires
    items.length < items.items.len;

// Remove and return the last element of a provably non-empty vector. The
// zero-case is a proof failure at the call site, not a runtime Option: stage
// 1 is an obligation, not a fallback — a
// `try_pop -> Optional<T>` belongs to the dynamic-failure stage-2 vocabulary.
boundary operator FixedVec::pop<T, const N: u64>(items: &mut FixedVec<T, N>) -> T
requires
    items.length > 0;

// Read the element at `index`, which must be provably within the live length.
boundary operator FixedVec::get<T, const N: u64>(items: &FixedVec<T, N>, index: u64) -> T
requires
    index < items.length;

// Borrow the LIVE elements as a slice view; the view length matches the
// vector length, not the capacity.
boundary operator FixedVec::as_slice<T, const N: u64>(items: &FixedVec<T, N>) -> &[T]
requires
    items.length <= items.items.len
ensures
    result.len == items.length;
